      "claim": "Delegated authority to subordinates does not create a new \"executive order\"; any such directive must still be issued (or signed) by the President.",
      "evidence": "Federal Register practice and the Administrative Procedure Act require that only the President can issue an executive order; delegated actions are labeled \"directives,\" \"memoranda,\" or \"regulations,\" not executive orders.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
